# Build output
bin/
# Anchored to the root, because each binary and its command's source
# directory share a name: an unanchored `aertun` would match cmd/aertun/ and
# git would silently refuse to add the source beside it.
/aertun
/aertun-collector
*.exe

# A store, which is data and not source. `aertun init` makes one in the
# working directory, so this is the file every contributor would otherwise
# commit on their first run.
.aertun/

# What a running site writes beside its store.
#
# Anchored to the root. Unanchored, "export/" matches a directory of that name
# at any depth — including internal/export, which is source: a new file there
# was invisible to git, silently, and only the already-tracked ones showed up.
# Same for templates, which is also the name of a directory a package could
# reasonably want.
/templates/
/export/
*.log

# Python packaging left over from an earlier life. Named rather than covered by
# a pattern, because the file that got committed declared a different licence
# from the one this project ships under and nobody noticed for months.
*.egg-info/

# Wheels and Python caches, which have no business in a project whose
# argument is that it has no third-party code.
*.whl
__pycache__/
